(
    version: 1,
    id: "m07",
    title: "Hold the Speed",
    chapter: 3,
    concepts: ["Feedback control", "P and PI"],
    world: (id: "ramp_lab", variant: Some("ramp_15")),
    robot: Starter("rover"),
    time_limit_s: 6.0,
    grading_seeds: [1, 2, 3],
    battery_soc: (0.6, 1.0),
    disabled_api: ["motor_speed", "motor_position"],
    brief_md: r#"
## Climb at a steady 0.25 m/s

Drive up the 15 degree ramp so that the **average speed between 2 s and 5 s is 0.25 m/s**, within
0.01 m/s.

This mission switches off `axl_motor_speed` and `axl_motor_position`: you build the controller
yourself. All you have is `axl_motor_power` and the encoder: `axl_motor_rate(motor)` gives the speed
of the wheel in radians per second.

Climbing slows the Rover down and a weak battery slows it down too. A controller measures the speed,
compares it with the goal, and corrects the power. That loop is called **feedback**.
"#,
    predict: Some((
        question: "At power 0.5 on both motors, how fast does the Rover climb the ramp between 2 s and 5 s?",
        unit: "m/s",
        measured: "ramp_speed_half_power_ms",
    )),
    criteria: [MeanSpeed(from_s: 2.0, to_s: 5.0, target_ms: 0.25, tol_ms: 0.01)],
    stars: (
        (text: "Within 0.005 m/s", criteria: [MeanSpeed(from_s: 2.0, to_s: 5.0, target_ms: 0.25, tol_ms: 0.005)]),
        (text: "Use axl_plot to see your controller work", criteria: [UsedPlot]),
    ),
    hints: (
        "Start with a fixed power that is about right, then add a correction that depends on the error: error = goal speed - measured speed.",
        "power = base + kp * error is a P controller. It always leaves a small error. Adding ki times the sum of past errors (a PI controller) removes it.",
        "Speed in m/s is the wheel rate in rad/s times the wheel radius 0.028 m. The encoder speed is coarse (steps of about 0.05 m/s), so average the two wheels and smooth it: speed = 0.8 * speed + 0.2 * new.",
    ),
)
